at are not about mixing with room air but about **speed and evaporation**: 1. **Forced Convection Accelerates Evaporative Cooling (Wind Chill)** Your skin is always losing a little moisture (insensible perspiration). A fast-moving jet of air—even warm air—strips away the humid boundary layer right at your skin’s surface much more efficiently. This massively accelerates the evaporation of that moisture. Evaporation is a cooling process; it sucks heat out of your skin. The air itself might be 35°C, but the *rate of heat removal* from your skin via rapid evaporation can make it feel cold. This is why a warm, windy day can make you feel chilly when you’re sweaty—the same principle on a tiny scale. 2. **Lower Heat Transfer Perceived (the “Sting” of a Jet)** A narrow, fast jet concentrates the airflow onto a tiny patch of skin. That high velocity triggers your skin’s mechanoreceptors (touch/pressure sensors) and cold-sensing thermoreceptors in a unique way. The sheer force of the jet can paradoxically make the temperature sensation skew “cool,” especially if the jet undergoes a very slight, real adiabatic expansion right at the lip opening. When you force air through a tight constriction, the pressure drops rapidly and the air expands. This expansion does drop the temperature a fraction of a degree right at the exit. It’s tiny—maybe a degree Celsius or two at most—but when combined with the intense evaporative cooling, your nerves definitely register it as a cool sensation. ### A Quick Test to Prove the Point Try this: purse your lips and blow a fast stream onto the back of a spoon or a piece of glass held right at your lips. The surface won’t cool down nearly as much as your skin does, because metal/glass doesn’t have sweat glands. You’re feeling the cooling of your *own skin* via evaporation, not a dramatic drop in the breath’s core temperature. So, to directly address your point: **The actual temperature of the breath leaving your mouth is almost the same in both cases